1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,972
An index is extra structure that helps the engine find rows without reading the whole table.

2
00:00:05,972 --> 00:00:10,041
Without one, a WHERE on email walks every customer.

3
00:00:10,041 --> 00:00:11,861
That is a sequential scan.

4
00:00:11,861 --> 00:00:13,305
Fine at four rows.

5
00:00:13,305 --> 00:00:15,041
Painful at four million.

6
00:00:15,041 --> 00:00:18,222
A B-tree keeps keys in sorted pages.

7
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:19,972
Lookups are logarithmic.

8
00:00:19,972 --> 00:00:25,611
Range queries work: email greater than m, placed_at last week.

9
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:28,875
The primary key usually already has an index.

10
00:00:28,875 --> 00:00:31,527
UNIQUE usually already has an index.

11
00:00:31,527 --> 00:00:35,000
You add more for the columns you filter and join on.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,555
orders.customer_id is a join column.

13
00:00:38,555 --> 00:00:42,944
Index it if the engine did not already, because of the foreign key.

14
00:00:42,944 --> 00:00:45,569
Some engines index foreign keys for you.

15
00:00:45,569 --> 00:00:46,763
Some do not.

16
00:00:46,763 --> 00:00:47,583
Check.

17
00:00:47,583 --> 00:00:53,958
A covering index includes the columns a query selects, so the engine can skip the table heap.

18
00:00:53,958 --> 00:00:55,791
That is a later tuning move.

19
00:00:55,791 --> 00:00:58,750
A partial index covers only some rows.

20
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,402
WHERE deleted_at IS NULL.

21
00:01:01,402 --> 00:01:03,930
Useful when most rows are archived.

22
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:05,833
Indexes cost writes.

23
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:09,750
Every INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE maintains them.

24
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:11,500
They also cost disk.

25
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,513
Do not index every column.

26
00:01:13,513 --> 00:01:18,361
Index the ones in WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY that actually run.

27
00:01:18,361 --> 00:01:20,638
EXPLAIN a query before you argue.

28
00:01:20,638 --> 00:01:25,347
If it already uses a primary key lookup, another index will not help.

29
00:01:25,347 --> 00:01:32,486
Low-cardinality columns like a boolean make weak indexes unless you combine them or use a partial index.

30
00:01:32,486 --> 00:01:35,305
Write CREATE INDEX with a name you can drop.

31
00:01:35,305 --> 00:01:38,763
orders_customer_id_idx.

32
00:01:38,763 --> 00:01:39,652
Measure.

33
00:01:39,652 --> 00:01:45,166
If a report is still slow, look at the query shape before adding a fifth index.

34
00:01:45,166 --> 00:01:48,291
Normalization and indexes are partners.

35
00:01:48,291 --> 00:01:51,138
A good key lets a small index do a lot.

36
00:01:51,138 --> 00:01:54,458
A wide, messy row makes a fat index.

37
00:01:54,458 --> 00:01:57,847
Find without a full scan, but only where you look.

38
00:01:57,847 --> 00:02:00,305
B-trees are not the only structure.

39
00:02:00,305 --> 00:02:05,625
Hash indexes exist for equality in some engines and not in SQLite.

40
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:10,611
GIN and GiST in PostgreSQL serve documents and geometry.

41
00:02:10,611 --> 00:02:14,541
You do not need them to understand why email should be indexed.

42
00:02:14,541 --> 00:02:17,472
You need them when you search inside JSON later.

43
00:02:17,472 --> 00:02:21,333
Clustered indexes in some engines are the table order.

44
00:02:21,333 --> 00:02:27,041
SQLite's WITHOUT ROWID tables make the primary key the table organization.

45
00:02:27,041 --> 00:02:30,402
That is physical design, after the logical key.

46
00:02:30,402 --> 00:02:33,625
Fillfactor and reindex are maintenance.

47
00:02:33,625 --> 00:02:38,055
VACUUM in SQLite reclaims space and can help the planner.

48
00:02:38,055 --> 00:02:39,583
That is a later how-to.

49
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:43,444
Here, know that indexes fragment under heavy writes.

50
00:02:43,444 --> 00:02:45,930
Measure again after a bulk load.

51
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:48,986
Composite indexes have a left-prefix rule.

52
00:02:48,986 --> 00:02:54,416
An index on city, last_name helps WHERE city equals Austin.

53
00:02:54,416 --> 00:03:00,194
It does not help WHERE last_name equals Maya as well as the other order would.

54
00:03:00,194 --> 00:03:06,569
Put equality columns that shrink the set first, in the way your queries actually filter.

55
00:03:06,569 --> 00:03:09,180
Do not guess the order without EXPLAIN.

56
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:11,888
Unused indexes still cost writes.

57
00:03:11,888 --> 00:03:14,208
Drop them when the query is gone.

58
00:03:14,208 --> 00:03:16,555
Name them so you can find who asked.

59
00:03:16,555 --> 00:03:19,069
A comment in the migration is enough.

60
00:03:19,069 --> 00:03:24,250
Find without a full scan, where you actually look, and not everywhere else.

61
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:27,958
That sentence is the whole index policy for this course.

62
00:03:27,958 --> 00:03:32,125
B-trees, JOIN columns, EXPLAIN, then stop.

63
00:03:32,125 --> 00:03:35,833
If you cannot name the query, you cannot name the index.

64
00:03:35,833 --> 00:03:37,458
Write the query first.

65
00:03:37,458 --> 00:03:38,513
EXPLAIN it.

66
00:03:38,513 --> 00:03:42,638
If it scans a toy table, wait until the table is not a toy.

67
00:03:42,638 --> 00:03:48,444
If it scans a large table on a column you filter every time, index that column.

68
00:03:48,444 --> 00:03:52,000
If it already seeks on the primary key, walk away.

69
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,916
If writes hurt after the fifth index, drop the one whose query retired.

70
00:03:56,916 --> 00:04:00,375
Names in migrations are how you find the retiree.

71
00:04:00,375 --> 00:04:03,166
B-trees for equality and ranges.

72
00:04:03,166 --> 00:04:05,347
Left prefix for composites.

73
00:04:05,347 --> 00:04:07,250
Partial for the live subset.

74
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:09,319
That is enough policy to start.

75
00:04:09,319 --> 00:04:13,166
Exotic index types can wait for exotic predicates.

76
00:04:13,166 --> 00:04:15,194
Most stores are WHERE and JOIN.

77
00:04:15,194 --> 00:04:17,597
Index those, then measure again.

78
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:25,305
An index is a lookup structure beside the table, like a book's table of contents, pointing at where a row lives.

79
00:04:25,305 --> 00:04:28,583
Without one, a search can walk every row.

80
00:04:28,583 --> 00:04:33,000
That is a sequential scan, and it gets slower as the table grows.

81
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,305
With a useful index, the engine jumps closer to the matches.

82
00:04:37,305 --> 00:04:43,541
A B-tree index keeps keys in a balanced tree so every lookup is about the same depth.

83
00:04:43,541 --> 00:04:52,736
Equality and ranges both like B-trees: customer_id equals seven, created_at greater than a date.

84
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:56,591
ORDER BY on an indexed column can avoid a separate sort.

85
00:04:56,591 --> 00:05:00,022
The tree is a copy of the key values plus pointers.

86
00:05:00,022 --> 00:05:05,980
Insert, update, and delete must maintain the tree, so writes get a little slower.

87
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,661
That is the tax you pay for faster reads.

88
00:05:08,661 --> 00:05:15,119
Create an index on columns you filter and join, not on every column because it felt complete.

89
00:05:15,119 --> 00:05:19,216
Each extra index is another structure to store and update.

90
00:05:19,216 --> 00:05:29,550
A composite index on (city, created_at) helps a filter that uses city, or city and created_at, left to right.

91
00:05:29,550 --> 00:05:35,063
It may not help a filter that only uses created_at, depending on the engine.

92
00:05:35,063 --> 00:05:38,313
Lead with the equality column you actually use.

93
00:05:38,313 --> 00:05:43,216
Primary keys and unique constraints usually already have an index.

94
00:05:43,216 --> 00:05:46,925
Do not invent a second one for the same columns without a reason.

95
00:05:46,925 --> 00:05:54,147
Full-text indexes are for language: words in a description, a body of text, a title search.

96
00:05:54,147 --> 00:06:00,744
A B-tree on a huge TEXT column is a poor match for find me reviews that mention delayed shipping.

97
00:06:00,744 --> 00:06:08,452
Full-text tokenization splits text into words, often ignores common words, and can rank by relevance.

98
00:06:08,452 --> 00:06:16,841
It is not the same as LIKE percent delayed percent, which cannot use a normal B-tree well when the pattern starts with a wildcard.

99
00:06:16,841 --> 00:06:23,661
Use B-tree for keys, dates, statuses, and the columns in JOIN and WHERE equality.

100
00:06:23,661 --> 00:06:26,550
Use full-text for natural language search.

101
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:33,452
Clustered versus heap storage, hash indexes, and covering indexes are extra costumes.

102
00:06:33,452 --> 00:06:38,147
Most application work is B-tree plus the occasional full-text.

103
00:06:38,147 --> 00:06:41,230
Prove it with EXPLAIN when a query is slow.

104
00:06:41,230 --> 00:06:48,744
If the plan still scans the heap of a large table, your WHERE is not using an index, or no index matches.

105
00:06:48,744 --> 00:06:53,286
If writes crawled after you added six indexes, you over-indexed.

106
00:06:53,286 --> 00:06:55,800
Index the questions you actually ask.

107
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,133
Not the columns you might someday filter.

108
00:06:58,133 --> 00:07:01,744
A bookstore needs title search and isbn lookup.

109
00:07:01,744 --> 00:07:05,355
It does not need an index on every descriptive adjective.

110
00:07:05,355 --> 00:07:08,619
Measure, then add, then measure again.

111
00:07:08,619 --> 00:07:11,397
The index is a tool, not a trophy.

112
00:07:11,397 --> 00:07:19,022
B-tree on orders.customer_id speeds WHERE customer_id equals and JOIN customers.

113
00:07:19,022 --> 00:07:23,369
B-tree on created_at speeds recent-order ranges.

114
00:07:23,369 --> 00:07:27,119
Full-text on reviews.body speeds language search.

115
00:07:27,119 --> 00:07:30,119
LIKE percent heat still struggles on a B-tree.

116
00:07:30,119 --> 00:07:32,855
A shopping app search box is full-text.

117
00:07:32,855 --> 00:07:35,966
An admin lookup by id is B-tree.

118
00:07:35,966 --> 00:07:46,355
EXPLAIN a slow list of a customer's orders; if it scans orders, add customer_id index unless it is already a foreign key with an index.

119
00:07:46,355 --> 00:07:48,980
Some engines index foreign keys for you.

120
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:50,175
Some do not.

121
00:07:50,175 --> 00:07:50,925
Look.

122
00:07:50,925 --> 00:07:55,647
Then stop adding indexes until the next slow query names a column.

123
00:07:55,647 --> 00:07:59,286
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

124
00:07:59,286 --> 00:08:02,341
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

125
00:08:02,341 --> 00:08:03,675
What is an index?

126
00:08:03,675 --> 00:08:06,133
A lookup structure beside the table.

127
00:08:06,133 --> 00:08:07,550
B-tree good for?

128
00:08:07,550 --> 00:08:11,258
Equality, ranges, many ORDER BY keys.

129
00:08:11,258 --> 00:08:12,688
Full-text good for?

130
00:08:12,688 --> 00:08:14,341
Natural language search.

131
00:08:14,341 --> 00:08:15,508
Write tax?

132
00:08:15,508 --> 00:08:17,647
Each index must be maintained.

133
00:08:17,647 --> 00:08:19,077
Index every column?

134
00:08:19,077 --> 00:08:19,841
No.

135
00:08:19,841 --> 00:08:21,813
Left prefix of composite?

136
00:08:21,813 --> 00:08:24,786
Helps filters that start from the leading column.

137
00:08:24,786 --> 00:08:26,119
LIKE percent word?

138
00:08:26,119 --> 00:08:28,022
B-tree will not help much.

139
00:08:28,022 --> 00:08:30,522
Primary key already indexed?

140
00:08:30,522 --> 00:08:31,480
Usually.

141
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:33,313
Foreign keys indexed?

142
00:08:33,313 --> 00:08:34,772
Depends on the engine.

143
00:08:34,772 --> 00:08:35,522
Look.

144
00:08:35,522 --> 00:08:36,369
Prove it?

145
00:08:36,369 --> 00:08:39,827
EXPLAIN, then add, then EXPLAIN again.

146
00:08:39,827 --> 00:08:44,550
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

